Original commit body from faee65043047c028a00328b09f4fa42c3f891ffe:

Taint and io-boundaries share one row choice (io_boundary.
gate_named_entry), which prefers a vouched row by the hit's `unvouched`
bit (ADR-0047). io-boundaries' IoPrimitive carries it. The taint rows
auto-derived from the same primitives did not: TaintSource had neither
`unvouched` nor `community`, and a TaintSink derived from a community
overlay was stamped community=False. So every taint hit sorted as
vouched, and the overlay row -- merged IN FRONT of the base -- won by
order. On dev 4892851370, `readProcess` after `import System.Process
(readProcess)` was the untrusted_input source
System.Process.Text.readProcess (process-extras, never imported) in
verify-claims while io-boundaries named System.Process.readProcess.
Traced at runtime: the call's module hint is `external`, so the GATE arm
chose the row; the hinted-path candidate was not reached.

Now a derived TaintSource / TaintSink carries its primitive's provenance
as `community`, community taint-file SOURCES are stamped as their sinks
and sanitizers already were, and both row types expose `unvouched` (=
community), the one attribute the gate sorts on. Derived sinks are all
trust_level untrusted, so `community` on them withholds no trust.

Re-run of the filed repro: the default run now names
System.Process.readProcess, as --no-default-overlays and io-boundaries
do; verdict `violated` unchanged.

